<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lothair II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 835–<a href="/years/869">869</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Lothair II of Lotharingia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> II was the second son of Emperor Lothair I and received the northern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-prum">Treaty of Prüm</a> in 855, a kingdom later named Lotharingia after him. His long attempt to divorce his wife Theutberga embroiled him with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. He died without legitimate heirs in 869, and his uncles divided his kingdom; the name survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lothair-ii.jpg" alt="Lothair II" width="236" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ekkehard von Aura · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lothar_II,_King_of_Lotharingie.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
